<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>At the centre of the case is a 3,500 square foot commercial unit at Worli, Mumbai, belonging to a partnership firm constituted under a 1985 deed that contains an arbitration clause. The appellants — admitted as partners under that deed — allege that a 1995 deed of retirement, by which they supposedly exited, is forged and does not bind them. The firm meanwhile passed into other hands: it was run as a proprietorship and, in 2009, two further partners were inducted under a fresh partnership deed. An earlier arbitral award the appellants had obtained was set aside under Section 34, and the parties returned to the Section 11 stage.</p>

<h2>What the High Court did, and how the Supreme Court modified it</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>High Court</h3><p>Decided limitation in the appellants\' favour as against the original partners, but declined to join the partners inducted in 2009 — non-signatories to the 1985 deed who, it held, fell outside the recognised exceptions.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>Supreme Court</h3><p>Joined the 2009-inducted partners as veritable parties: the dispute over the firm\'s asset and true constitution could affect their rights under their own deed, so they belong before the tribunal — on a prima facie basis only.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The doctrinal thread</h2>
<p>The bench of Justices J.B. Pardiwala and K. Vinod Chandran drew on <em>Cox and Kings Ltd. v. SAP India Pvt. Ltd.</em>, which re-founded the group-of-companies doctrine on consent-in-substance, and on subsequent authority applying that framework at the referral stage. The operative idea is composite: where a transaction or asset is one, and a stranger to the signature page is so interlaced with it that relief cannot be moulded without touching their rights, the referral court should not amputate them from the arbitration at the threshold. The tribunal — not the Section 11 judge — then decides finally who is bound.</p>

<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step one</strong> — identify the arbitration agreement\'s existence: here, the 1985 deed\'s clause, prima facie engaged by partners claiming under the firm.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step two</strong> — ask whether the non-signatory is arguably a veritable party by relationship, conduct and stake in the subject matter.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step three</strong> — refer, leaving jurisdiction, limitation, estoppel and merits to the tribunal under Section 16.</div>
</div>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>Drafting and strategy notes:</strong> partnership deeds should state how the arbitration clause operates on reconstitution, retirement and induction of partners; parties resisting joinder should build their record on absence of consent-in-substance, not merely absence of signature; and claimants should plead the composite character of the transaction with specificity at the Section 11 stage, since that is what carries a non-signatory across the threshold.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>For family firms — where deeds multiply across generations and the firm\'s assets stay constant — the decision matters. A partner cannot insulate the firm\'s principal asset from arbitration by pointing to a later deed, and equally, later-inducted partners cannot be condemned unheard: they are brought in precisely so that the tribunal\'s eventual award binds a complete set of parties.</p>
</div>
